{% psec 1, "Pricing Coffee Derivatives on a Commodities Desk", "What was the brief?", "Overview" %}
<p class="topic-intro">
  Three tasks from Citi's Markets Quant job simulation, completed in July 2026. I priced a six-month coffee option three different ways, used the same engine to design a coffee-linked structured note for a client, and set out how a desk should manage the market, credit and operational risks around it. Everything below was built in Python and checked by re-running the code.
</p>
<div class="proj-links">
  <a class="proj-link" href="https://github.com/tamaraabu4-cell/heritage-edge/blob/main/src/projects/coffee_option_pricing.py" target="_blank">View code on GitHub &nearr;</a>
</div>

<div class="metric-row">
  <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Fair value of the call</div><div class="metric-val">\(\$0.0712\)/lb</div><div class="metric-sub">\(\approx \$2{,}670\) per contract</div></div>
  <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Monte Carlo vs formula</div><div class="metric-val">\(0.02\%\)</div><div class="metric-sub">difference, 500k paths</div></div>
  <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Note participation</div><div class="metric-val">\(40.9\%\)</div><div class="metric-sub">\(3\%\) coupon, \(80\%\) barrier</div></div>
  <div class="metric"><div class="metric-lbl">Tail risk removed</div><div class="metric-val">\(85\%\)</div><div class="metric-sub">by a protective put</div></div>
</div>

<div class="sh">In plain words</div>
<p class="np">An <strong>option</strong> is like insurance. A coffee call option gives you the right, but not the obligation, to buy coffee at a fixed price later. If coffee gets expensive, the insurance pays out; if it doesn't, you've only lost the premium. The hard part is deciding what that insurance is <strong>worth today</strong>, and how much risk you carry once you've sold it to someone.</p>

<div class="summary-grid">
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">1</div><div class="sum-title">Price the option</div><div class="sum-body">Fair futures price from <strong>cost of carry</strong>, option value from <strong>Black-76</strong>, then a <strong>Monte Carlo</strong> simulation to check the answer and measure tail risk.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon blue">2</div><div class="sum-title">Build a structured note</div><div class="sum-body">A product paying a coupon plus coffee upside. Solved for the participation rate that makes it worth exactly what the client pays, and showed the client's <strong>\(26.6\%\) chance of losing money</strong>.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon amber">3</div><div class="sum-title">Manage the risks</div><div class="sum-body">Hedging a weather view, onboarding a risky supplier, and fixing manual trade errors &mdash; each sized before it is mitigated.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon purple">4</div><div class="sum-title">Know the model's limits</div><div class="sum-body">A single flat volatility misprices out-of-the-money options. In production I'd price off the <strong>implied volatility surface</strong>.</div></div>
</div>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 2, "Three Models, One Answer", "What is the option worth?", "Pricing the Option" %}
<p class="topic-intro">The desk needed a fair value for a six-month call on coffee struck at \(\$1.25\) per pound. Rather than trust one model, I chained three together so each one checks the next.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">01</div><div class="fs-t">Cost of carry</div><div class="fs-b">What should coffee for delivery in \(6\) months cost? <strong>F = \(\$1.2181\)</strong></div></div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">02</div><div class="fs-t">Black-76</div><div class="fs-b">What is the insurance on that future worth? <strong>\(\$0.0712\)/lb</strong></div></div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="flow-step"><div class="fs-n">03</div><div class="fs-t">Monte Carlo</div><div class="fs-b">Simulate \(500{,}000\) futures and check: <strong>\(\$0.0712\) &plusmn; \(0.0004\)</strong></div></div>
</div>

<div class="sh">Step 1 &mdash; the fair futures price</div>
<p class="lc-q">Why isn't coffee for later delivery the same price as coffee today?</p>
<p class="np">Because holding coffee costs money. If you bought beans today to sell in six months, you'd pay interest on the money you borrowed and pay to store them. So the price for later delivery must include those costs, or someone could lock in a free profit.</p>
<div class="f-label">Cost of carry &mdash; spot plus the cost of holding it</div>
<div class="f-box">\[ F = S_0 \times e^{(r + u - y)T} = 1.20 \times e^{0.015} = \$1.2181 \]</div>
<table class="vt">
  <tr><td>\(S_0\)</td><td>Spot price today &mdash; \(\$1.20\)/lb</td></tr>
  <tr><td>\(r\)</td><td>Interest rate &mdash; \(2\%\) a year</td></tr>
  <tr><td>\(u\)</td><td>Storage cost &mdash; \(1\%\) a year</td></tr>
  <tr><td>\(y\)</td><td>Convenience yield &mdash; the benefit of physically having beans (not given, set to \(0\) and stress-tested)</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="sh">Step 2 &mdash; Black-76 and the Greeks</div>
<p class="np">Black-76 is the version of Black-Scholes used for options on <strong>futures</strong>. Drag the sliders to see how the option's value responds. The gap between the curve and the dashed line is <strong>time value</strong> &mdash; what you pay for the chance that coffee rises before expiry.</p>

<div class="chart-block">
  <div class="chart-block-title">Call Value vs Futures Price &mdash; Strike $1.25</div>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartB76"></canvas></div>
