  </div>
</div>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">What the Greeks mean for a trader</div>
  <strong>Delta (\(0.47\))</strong>: hedge each option with about half a futures contract. <strong>Gamma (\(1.83\))</strong>: that hedge ratio moves quickly, so it needs frequent rebalancing. <strong>Vega</strong>: one extra point of volatility adds about \(\$128\) per contract. <strong>Theta</strong>: every day that passes, the option loses a little value.
</div>

<div class="sh">Step 3 &mdash; Monte Carlo as a cross-check</div>
<p class="np">Monte Carlo prices an option by brute force: simulate thousands of possible futures prices at expiry, work out the payoff in each, and average them. On a simple option it should match the formula. If it does, you can trust the same engine on complicated products where no formula exists. That's exactly what Task \(2\) needed.</p>

<div class="chart-block">
  <div class="chart-block-title">Simulated Coffee Futures Price at Expiry</div>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartMC"></canvas></div>
  <div class="chart-controls">
    <span class="cc-label" id="mc-out" style="min-width:0;flex:1">&ndash;</span>
    <button class="mc-btn" onclick="runMC()">Run again &#8635;</button>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="diagram-cap">This in-browser version runs \(20{,}000\) paths for speed. The Python model uses \(500{,}000\) antithetic paths and lands within \(0.02\%\) of Black-76, with \(40.7\%\) of paths finishing above the strike.</p>

<div class="sh">How real-world shocks reprice the option</div>
<p class="np">A model is only as good as its inputs. I mapped real events &mdash; frost, drought, shipping disruption &mdash; onto the inputs and re-priced the option each time.</p>
<div class="chart-block">
  <div class="chart-block-title">Call Value Under Each Scenario ($/lb) &mdash; base case $0.0712</div>
  <div class="chart-wrap" style="height:320px"><canvas id="chartSc"></canvas></div>
</div>
<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">The key finding</div>
  The shocks are <strong>lopsided</strong>. A Brazilian frost lifts the call by <strong>\(+366\%\)</strong>, because it pushes up the price <em>and</em> the volatility at the same time &mdash; delta and vega add together. A bumper crop takes it down \(90\%\), but an option can't fall below zero. Long options are the natural way to express weather risk.
</div>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 3, "Designing a Coffee-Linked Note", "How do you build a client product around it?", "Structured Note" %}
<p class="topic-intro">The client wanted two things that pull against each other: steady income, like a bond, and a share of any rise in coffee prices, like an option. A structured note combines both &mdash; but every feature has to be paid for by another.</p>

<div class="sh">A note is just three simpler pieces</div>
<div class="f-box">\[ \text{Note} = \text{Zero-coupon bond} + \alpha \times \text{Call} - \text{Down-and-in put} \]</div>
<table class="vt">
  <tr><td>Bond</td><td>Returns the client's money at maturity &mdash; the safe part</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Call</td><td>The client's share \((\alpha)\) of any coffee upside</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Put</td><td>The client <em>sells</em> protection below an \(80\%\) barrier; that premium funds the coupon and upside</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="worked">
  <div class="worked-title">Nothing is free &mdash; the funding constraint</div>
  <div class="worked-body">At a \(2\%\) interest rate, guaranteeing \(\$1{,}000\) back in two years costs:
    <div class="worked-math tex">\[ \begin{aligned}
\text{Bond} &= 1{,}000 \times e^{-0.02 \times 2} = \$960.79 \\
\text{Left to buy upside} &= 1{,}000 - 960.79 = \$39.21 \;\rightarrow\; \text{only } 23\% \text{ participation}
\end{aligned} \]</div>
    To give the client a coupon <em>and</em> real upside, they have to give something up. The \(80\%\) barrier is that trade-off: it's a put the client sells, and its premium lifts participation to \(40.9\%\).</div>
</div>

<div class="dt-wrap"><table class="dt">
  <tr><th>Structure</th><th>Coupon</th><th>Downside</th><th>Participation &alpha;</th><th>P(loss)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>A. Full protection</td><td>none</td><td>Protected</td><td>\(23.0\%\)</td><td>\(0.0\%\)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>B. Full protection, capped \(+40\%\)</td><td>none</td><td>Protected</td><td>\(32.8\%\)</td><td>\(0.0\%\)</td></tr>
  <tr class="hl"><td>C. Recommended</td><td>\(3\%\) p.a.</td><td>\(80\%\) barrier</td><td>\(40.9\%\)</td><td>\(26.6\%\)</td></tr>
  <tr><td>D. Aggressive coupon</td><td>\(5\%\) p.a.</td><td>\(70\%\) barrier, cap \(+50\%\)</td><td>\(4.1\%\)</td><td>\(15.8\%\)</td></tr>
</table></div>

<div class="chart-block">
  <div class="chart-block-title">Payoff at Maturity on a $1,000 Note (Structure C)</div>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartNote"></canvas></div>
</div>
<p class="diagram-cap">Above \(\$1.20\) the client keeps \(40.9\%\) of the upside plus the \(\$60\) coupon. Between the barrier and \(\$1.20\) they get \(\$1{,}060\) back. Below \(\$0.96\) they lose one-for-one with coffee.</p>

<div class="insight">
  <div class="insight-lbl">The disclosure that matters</div>
  Sold as "\(80\%\) protected", the note still has a <strong>\(26.6\%\) chance of losing the client money</strong> in my 400,000-path simulation, with a 5th-percentile payoff of \(\$618\). A single price can't show that; the full distribution can. That's why the Monte Carlo output belongs in the client documents.
</div>

<div class="sh">The risk Citi keeps after selling it</div>
<p class="np">Selling the note leaves the bank short the payoff. The hardest part is <strong>gamma near the barrier</strong>: as coffee falls towards \(\$0.96\), the hedge changes sharply, forcing the desk to trade heavily in a falling, nervous market. That cost doesn't appear in the note's theoretical value, so it has to be priced in.</p>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 4, "You Can't Hedge What You Haven't Measured", "What risk does the desk carry after selling it?", "Risk Management" %}
<p class="topic-intro">Three risk scenarios, one framework: identify the exposure, size it, mitigate it, then monitor it.</p>

<div class="chart-block">
  <div class="chart-block-title">Holding One Contract of Coffee (37,500 lb) &mdash; Worst-Case Losses</div>
  <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartRisk"></canvas></div>
</div>
<p class="np">From the Task \(1\) simulation: buying a \(\$1.25\) put costs <strong>\(\$3{,}853\)</strong> (\(8.6\%\) of the position's value) and cuts the average loss in the worst \(1\%\) of outcomes from <strong>\(\$17{,}544\) to \(\$2{,}658\)</strong>. I reported Expected Shortfall alongside VaR because coffee has fat tails &mdash; VaR tells you where the bad zone starts, ES tells you how bad it gets.</p>

<div class="summary-grid">
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon green">M</div><div class="sum-title">Market risk &mdash; a weather view</div><div class="sum-body">First establish which side you're on. For a buyer, I recommended a <strong>collar or a \(60\text{–}80\%\) futures hedge</strong>, sized with the minimum-variance hedge ratio, because the weather view may already be in the price.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon blue">C</div><div class="sum-title">Credit risk &mdash; a new supplier</div><div class="sum-body">The danger is <strong>wrong-way risk</strong>: a drought makes the supplier likely to default exactly when replacing their coffee is most expensive. Collateral is the one fix that still works then.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon amber">O</div><div class="sum-title">Operational risk &mdash; manual errors</div><div class="sum-body">Diagnose the root cause, then <strong>automate</strong> execution to settlement. Checks and approvals are back-ups for a manual step that shouldn't exist.</div></div>
  <div class="sum-card"><div class="sum-icon purple">&Sigma;</div><div class="sum-title">The common thread</div><div class="sum-body">Hedging doesn't remove basis risk; collateral doesn't remove wrong-way risk; automation moves risk into the system. Risk management is choosing <strong>which</strong> risks to keep, and at what price.</div></div>
</div>
{% endpsec %}

{% psec 5, "Where the Model Breaks", "Where does it fall short, and what would I do next?", "Limits and Next Steps" %}
<p class="topic-intro">The maths was the easy part. The real risk sits in the assumptions, and these are the ones I'd change first on a real desk.</p>

<div class="sh">1. One volatility number isn't enough</div>
<p class="np">I used a flat \(25\%\) volatility for every strike. Real markets don't: coffee traders pay extra for out-of-the-money calls because frost risk only pushes prices one way. A flat-vol model therefore <strong>under-prices the call</strong> in Task \(1\) and the barrier put in Task \(2\) &mdash; meaning the note would be sold too cheaply. The fix is to price off the <strong>implied volatility surface</strong>.</p>

<div class="sh">2. Prices don't move smoothly</div>
<p class="np">The simulation assumes prices drift and wobble smoothly. Coffee jumps: a frost can move it \(30\%\) in a week. A <strong>mean-reverting jump-diffusion</strong> model would show barrier breaches and tail losses more honestly.</p>

<div class="sh">3. The hedge has costs the price ignores</div>
<p class="np">Gamma near the barrier, margin calls on a working futures hedge, and basis risk between physical beans and the exchange contract all cost money. None of them show up in a theoretical value, so they need explicit reserves.</p>

<div class="sh">The core code</div>
<div class="code">def black76(F, K, r, T, sigma):
    """Call, put and Greeks for an option on a futures contract."""
    sqrtT = np.sqrt(T)
    d1 = (np.log(F / K) + 0.5 * sigma**2 * T) / (sigma * sqrtT)
    d2 = d1 - sigma * sqrtT
    disc = np.exp(-r * T)
    call = disc * (F * norm.cdf(d1) - K * norm.cdf(d2))
    put  = disc * (K * norm.cdf(-d2) - F * norm.cdf(-d1))
    delta = disc * norm.cdf(d1)
    gamma = disc * norm.pdf(d1) / (F * sigma * sqrtT)
    vega  = disc * F * norm.pdf(d1) * sqrtT / 100      # per vol point
    return call, put, delta, gamma, vega</div>

<div class="qbox">
  <div class="qbox-lbl">Skills used</div>
  <p class="qbox-body">Pricing an option is half the job; knowing how far to trust the price is the other half.</p>
  <span class="qtag">Black-76</span><span class="qtag">Greeks</span><span class="qtag">Monte Carlo</span><span class="qtag">Antithetic variates</span><span class="qtag">Structured products</span><span class="qtag">VaR &amp; ES</span><span class="qtag">Root finding (brentq)</span><span class="qtag">NumPy / SciPy / pandas</span>
</div>
{% endpsec %}
<script>
const $=id=>document.getElementById(id);
const isDark=()=>document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-theme')==='dark';
const charts={};


function getColors(){
  return {
    grid:isDark()?'rgba(255,255,255,0.05)':'rgba(0,0,0,0.04)',
    text:isDark()?'#636366':'#aeaeb2',
    tooltipBg:isDark()?'#1e1e25':'#fff',
    tooltipText:isDark()?'#f5f5f7':'#1d1d1f',
    tooltipBorder:isDark()?'rgba(255,255,255,0.1)':'rgba(0,0,0,0.1)'
  };
}
function chartOpts(yFmt,xFmt,extra){
  const c=getColors();
  const o={
    responsive:true,maintainAspectRatio:false,animation:{duration:250},
    plugins:{
      legend:{position:'top',align:'center',labels:{color:c.text,font:{size:11,family:'DM Sans'},usePointStyle:true,pointStyleWidth:10,padding:14,boxHeight:8}},
      tooltip:{backgroundColor:c.tooltipBg,titleColor:c.text,bodyColor:c.tooltipText,borderColor:c.tooltipBorder,borderWidth:1,padding:12}
    },
    scales:{
      x:{grid:{color:c.grid},ticks:{color:c.text,font:{size:10,family:'DM Sans'},maxTicksLimit:9,callback:xFmt},border:{display:false}},
      y:{grid:{color:c.grid},ticks:{color:c.text,font:{size:10,family:'DM Sans'},callback:yFmt},border:{display:false}}
    }
  };
  return Object.assign(o,extra||{});
}

// ── maths ──
function ncdf(x){const t=1/(1+0.2316419*Math.abs(x));const d=0.3989423*Math.exp(-x*x/2);
  const p=d*t*(0.3193815+t*(-0.3565638+t*(1.781478+t*(-1.821256+t*1.330274))));return x>0?1-p:p;}
function npdf(x){return Math.exp(-x*x/2)/Math.sqrt(2*Math.PI);}
const K=1.25,R=0.02,F0=1.20*Math.exp(0.015);
function b76(F,sig,T){
  const s=Math.sqrt(T),d1=(Math.log(F/K)+0.5*sig*sig*T)/(sig*s),d2=d1-sig*s,df=Math.exp(-R*T);
  const call=df*(F*ncdf(d1)-K*ncdf(d2));
  return {call,delta:df*ncdf(d1),vega:df*F*npdf(d1)*s/100,
    theta:(-df*F*npdf(d1)*sig/(2*s)+R*call)/365,pex:ncdf(d2)};
}

// ── Black-76 interactive ──
const fGrid=Array.from({length:61},(_,i)=>+(0.95+i*0.01).toFixed(2));
function b76Data(sig,T,F){
  return {
    labels:fGrid,
    datasets:[
      {label:'Value today',data:fGrid.map(f=>b76(f,sig,T).call),borderColor:'#059669',borderWidth:2.5,pointRadius:0,tension:0.3},
      {label:'Payoff at expiry',data:fGrid.map(f=>Math.max(f-K,0)),borderColor:'#dc2626',borderWidth:1.8,borderDash:[5,4],pointRadius:0,tension:0},
      {label:'Current futures price',data:fGrid.map(f=>Math.abs(f-F)<0.005?b76(f,sig,T).call:null),borderColor:'#d97706',backgroundColor:'#d97706',pointRadius:6,showLine:false}
    ]
  };
}
function updB76(){
  const F=+$('b-f').value,sig=$('b-v').value/100,m=+$('b-t').value,T=m/12;
  $("b-f-v").textContent="$"+F.toFixed(4);$('b-v-v').textContent=Math.round(sig*100)+'%';$('b-t-v').textContent=m;
  const g=b76(F,sig,T);
  $('o-c').textContent='$'+g.call.toFixed(4);$('o-d').textContent=g.delta.toFixed(2);
  $('o-v').textContent=g.vega.toFixed(4);$('o-t').textContent=g.theta.toFixed(5);
  $('o-p').textContent=(g.pex*100).toFixed(1)+'%';
  const d=b76Data(sig,T,F);
  if(charts.b76){charts.b76.data=d;charts.b76.update();}
  else charts.b76=new Chart($('chartB76'),{type:'line',data:d,options:chartOpts(v=>'$'+(+v).toFixed(2),function(v){return '$'+this.getLabelForValue(v)},{interaction:{intersect:false,mode:'index'}})});
}
['b-f','b-v','b-t'].forEach(id=>$(id).addEventListener('input',updB76));

// ── Monte Carlo ──
let seed=42;
function rng(){seed|=0;seed=seed+0x6D2B79F5|0;let t=Math.imul(seed^seed>>>15,1|seed);t=t+Math.imul(t^t>>>7,61|t)^t;return((t^t>>>14)>>>0)/4294967296;}
function runMC(){
  const n=20000,sig=0.25,T=0.5,FT=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n/2;i++){
    const z=Math.sqrt(-2*Math.log(rng()||1e-12))*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*rng());
    [z,-z].forEach(x=>FT.push(F0*Math.exp(-0.5*sig*sig*T+sig*Math.sqrt(T)*x)));
  }
  const pay=FT.reduce((a,f)=>a+Math.max(f-K,0),0)/n*Math.exp(-R*T);
  const itm=FT.filter(f=>f>K).length/n;
  const lo=0.6,hi=2.0,bins=56,w=(hi-lo)/bins,counts=new Array(bins).fill(0);
  FT.forEach(f=>{const b=Math.floor((f-lo)/w);if(b>=0&&b<bins)counts[b]++;});
  const labels=counts.map((_,i)=>(lo+(i+0.5)*w).toFixed(2));
  const cols=labels.map(l=>+l>K?'rgba(5,150,105,0.75)':'rgba(148,163,184,0.55)');
  const d={labels,datasets:[{label:'Simulated paths (green = finishes above $1.25 strike)',data:counts,backgroundColor:cols,borderWidth:0,barPercentage:1,categoryPercentage:1}]};
  if(charts.mc){charts.mc.data=d;charts.mc.update();}
  else charts.mc=new Chart($('chartMC'),{type:'bar',data:d,options:chartOpts(v=>v,function(v){return '$'+this.getLabelForValue(v)})});
  $('mc-out').innerHTML='Monte Carlo call: <strong style="color:var(--tx1)">$'+pay.toFixed(4)+'</strong> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; Black-76: <strong style="color:var(--tx1)">$0.0712</strong> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; finishing in the money: <strong style="color:var(--tx1)">'+(itm*100).toFixed(1)+'%</strong>';
}

// ── Scenarios ──
function buildSc(){
  const sc=[['Brazil frost',0.3320],['Drought / poor harvest',0.1932],['Export ban / tariff',0.1887],['Red Sea freight',0.1205],['Rates spike to 6%',0.0817],['Base case',0.0712],['Recession demand slump',0.0348],['Bumper crop',0.0070]];
  charts.sc=new Chart($('chartSc'),{type:'bar',
    data:{labels:sc.map(s=>s[0]),datasets:[{label:'Call value ($/lb)',data:sc.map(s=>s[1]),
      backgroundColor:sc.map(s=>s[0]==='Base case'?'#2563eb':s[1]>0.0712?'rgba(5,150,105,0.8)':'rgba(220,38,38,0.75)'),borderWidth:0,borderRadius:4}]},
    options:(()=>{const o=chartOpts(function(v){return this.getLabelForValue(v)},v=>'$'+(+v).toFixed(2));o.indexAxis='y';o.plugins.legend.display=false;return o})()});
}

// ── Note payoff ──
function buildNote(){
  const a=0.409,cpn=60,bar=0.96,xs=[];for(let s=0.5;s<=2.0001;s+=0.01)xs.push(+s.toFixed(2));
  const note=xs.map(s=>({x:s,y:(s<bar?1000*s/1.2:1000)+1000*a*Math.max(s/1.2-1,0)+cpn}));
  const direct=xs.map(s=>({x:s,y:1000*s/1.2}));
  charts.note=new Chart($('chartNote'),{type:'line',
    data:{datasets:[
      {label:'Note payoff',data:note,borderColor:'#059669',borderWidth:2.5,pointRadius:0,tension:0},
      {label:'Owning coffee directly',data:direct,borderColor:'#dc2626',borderWidth:1.8,borderDash:[5,4],pointRadius:0},
      {label:'Barrier $0.96',data:[{x:0.96,y:300},{x:0.96,y:1800}],borderColor:'#d97706',borderWidth:1.5,borderDash:[3,3],pointRadius:0}
    ]},
    options:(()=>{const o=chartOpts(v=>'$'+Math.round(v),v=>'$'+(+v).toFixed(2));Object.assign(o.scales.x,{type:'linear',min:0.5,max:2.0});return o})()});
}

// ── Risk ──
function buildRisk(){
  charts.risk=new Chart($('chartRisk'),{type:'bar',
    data:{labels:['99% VaR','99% Expected Shortfall'],datasets:[
      {label:'Unhedged',data:[15862,17544],backgroundColor:'rgba(220,38,38,0.75)',borderRadius:4},
      {label:'With protective put',data:[2658,2658],backgroundColor:'rgba(5,150,105,0.8)',borderRadius:4}]},
    options:chartOpts(v=>'$'+(+v).toLocaleString())});
}

function buildAll(){
  Object.keys(charts).forEach(k=>{charts[k].destroy();delete charts[k];});
  updB76();runMC();buildSc();buildNote();buildRisk();
}
// Redraw the charts in the right colours when the site's theme changes
document.addEventListener('themechange',()=>{seed=42;buildAll();});

buildAll();
</script>
